# 2026-10-09 -- second LOCAL patch, independent of the linux-surface rebase:
# surface-rtc-ec-clock.patch. pkgrel bumped 2 -> 3 so the builder rebuilds
# cachyos-7.2.9-2 with it. (First pushed as 2abd055 on a wrong assumption,
# corrected the same day before any build; see "The firmware's rule" below.)
#
# Why: on the Surface Laptop 4 AMD the clock comes up 1h57m fast at every
# boot, though Linux keeps the CMOS RTC on UTC all session:
#
#   rtc_cmos rtc_cmos: setting system clock to 2026-10-09T11:33:29 UTC
#   (real UTC at that moment, from timesyncd's first sync: 09:36:27)
#
# Anything that runs before timesyncd syncs sees that, and it broke WireGuard:
# a handshake sent with the clock ahead stores a future timestamp on the peer,
# which then drops every later handshake as a replay until real time catches
# up.
#
# Cause: nothing written to rtc0 survives a power cycle; the firmware rebuilds
# the CMOS RTC at POST from the EC's own clock (linux-surface #415, #1658,
# #1497, #2251). The ACPI clock device that should reach the EC, \_SB.SRTC
# (ACPI000E, _SUB MSHW0214), declares get/set-time support in _GCP but has no
# _GRT/_SRT (linux-surface/acpidumps surface_laptop_4_amd), so acpi-tad's rtc1
# returns EIO on every access. linux-surface's rtc-surface driver reaches the
# EC clock over SAM (TC 0x01 TID 0x01, CID 0x10 get / 0x0f set, le32 unix
# time) and IS built here (surface.config sets RTC_DRV_SURFACE=m, rebased.patch
# adds the file), but no registry group instantiates its device,
# ssam:01:01:01:00:00, so it never binds.
#
# The firmware's rule, measured on monketop (read through
# surface_aggregator_cdev):
#
#   EC clock                       = real UTC + 9h57m02s
#   EFI var RTC-378d7b65-... (EDK2 PcRtc) TimeZone = 480 min, Daylight 0
#   CMOS at POST, two boots        = real UTC + 1h57m02s / + 1h57m01s
#
#   => CMOS_at_POST = EC - TimeZone, to the second.
#
# So the EC is not "the time": the firmware subtracts its TimeZone from it.
# (Windows, set to Swedish local time, wrote the EC through the firmware,
# which added 480 min -> EC ~10 h ahead; drifted 3 min since.) The 2 h CEST
# offset this first looked like was a coincidence. Writing plain UTC to the EC
# -- what 2abd055 did -- would have made every boot come up 8 h BEHIND.
#
# The patch:
#   - surface_aggregator_registry.c: add ssam_node_rtc to ssam_node_group_sl3
#     (Surface Laptop 3 and 4; MSHW0110 is the SL4 AMD). rtc-surface then
#     binds and the EC clock shows up as an extra /dev/rtcN.
#   - rtc-surface.c: at probe, read TimeZone from the PcRtc "RTC" EFI variable
#     (0 if missing or 2047/unspecified) and apply it on every read and set,
#     so the RTC device speaks UTC and a write of UTC makes the NEXT boot's
#     CMOS UTC. Logs "EC clock kept N min ahead of UTC" when it is nonzero.
#     Plus a driver .shutdown that writes the system time that way: the
#     kernel's 11-minute NTP sync only writes RTC_SYSTOHC_DEVICE (rtc0, the
#     CMOS), so without it the EC never learns the corrected time. The SSAM
#     bus has no .shutdown of its own, so the driver core calls this one, and
#     devices probed later shut down first, so it runs while the SAM
#     controller is still up.
#
# Checked before pushing:
#   - Generated with diff -U3 against CachyOS's own files at tag
#     cachyos-7.2.9-2 with rebased.patch's registry and rtc-surface hunks
#     applied. Applies with -F0, no fuzz, no offset.
#   - Applied AFTER the Surface patch block in prepare(), because it edits a
#     file rebased.patch creates, and so it can never make rebased.patch fail.
#     -F0 dry-run gate as for amd-pstate: if it stops applying, prepare()
#     prints ":: surface-rtc: ... building without it" and carries on. Both
#     paths were run under set -eE with an ERR trap, as makepkg runs prepare().
#   - Both files compiled with clang (LLVM=1, W=1) against the installed
#     7.2.9-2-cachyos-surface-latest headers: no errors, no warnings; modpost
#     resolves efi and the SSAM symbols.
#
# VALIDATED 2026-10-09 on monketop: built and booted as 7.2.9-3 (from
# d936637). surface_rtc binds as rtc2 and logs "EC clock kept 480 min ahead of
# UTC (firmware TimeZone)". CMOS at boot vs real UTC (from timesyncd's first
# sync), per boot:
#
#   7.2.9-2                          +7021 s   (1h57m fast, the bug)
#   7.2.9-3, first boot              +7021 s   (EC not corrected yet; its
#                                              shutdown is what writes it)
#   7.2.9-3, second boot               -1.6 s
#
# So the firmware's rule (CMOS at POST = EC - TimeZone) holds, and each
# shutdown now hands the next boot the right time. -1.6 s is the CMOS RTC's
# whole-second resolution plus the shutdown-to-POST gap.
#
# To check it again:
#   journalctl -b -k | grep -E 'EC clock kept|setting system clock'
# If 'setting system clock' is ever ~8 h off in either direction after a
# shutdown on this kernel, the firmware's TimeZone rule has changed.

# 2026-10-14 -- context-only refresh for cachyos-7.2.10-1. No semantic change.
